{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler who reigned from 1130 to 1142. He was a prominent figure in the Crusader states and a key player in the political landscape of the Levant. His reign marked a period of both prosperity and conflict for the County of Tripoli.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county after his father's death in 1130. His early reign was characterized by consolidating his power and dealing with internal rebellions. He faced opposition from various factions within the county seeking to undermine his authority. Raymond proved to be a capable administrator and military leader quickly establishing his dominance. He strengthened the county's defenses and expanded its territory.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw significant military activity. He engaged in several campaigns against Muslim forces primarily in Syria. These campaigns were often costly and resulted in territorial losses. However Raymond also successfully defended the county against various incursions. He maintained a strong alliance with the Kingdom of Jerusalem which was crucial for the county’s survival. Raymond’s military successes helped to solidify Tripoli's position as a major power in the region. He also fostered trade and economic growth within the county.\n\n*Political and Military Campaigns* Raymond III’s reign was marked by frequent conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim powers. He launched several expeditions into Syria attempting to regain lost territories and establish a buffer zone. These campaigns were often marked by setbacks and heavy losses. He also engaged in conflicts with the Normans of Antioch and other rival powers. Raymond skillfully navigated the complex political landscape of the Levant forging alliances and maintaining a balance of power. He was known for his diplomatic skills and his ability to negotiate treaties with various factions. His military campaigns while often unsuccessful in achieving lasting gains were essential for protecting Tripoli’s borders and maintaining its independence.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III’s reign ended in 1142 when he died at the age of 52. His death marked the end of a period of relative stability and prosperity for Tripoli. His successor struggled to maintain the county's strength and its position in the region. Raymond III is remembered as a valiant ruler who defended the county against numerous enemies. He is regarded as one of the most successful counts of Tripoli and a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states. His legacy continues to be debated by historians who examine his military achievements political strategies and impact on the region. He left behind a legacy of resilience and determination that shaped the future of the County of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.14342188835144043}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.5060096454108134e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0798519179224968}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.04111293703317642}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5501455068588257}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.6038829684257507}, {"token": "▁who", "logprob": -0.6161623597145081}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -0.5125678777694702}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -1.3284698724746704}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.0020624573808163404}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00010442188795423135}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0009444067254662514}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -2.223893880844116}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -0.008467486128211021}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.01970384642481804}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.0005759726045653224}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -